WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.936
This is Chapter Two, Episode Four, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:10.636
Now: how does democracy work, day to day?

4
00:00:10.586 --> 00:00:16.186
Danish democracy is clearly expressed when the Folketing discusses and handles a bill.

5
00:00:16.186 --> 00:00:20.323
It is here that the parties show their different political views and interests.

6
00:00:20.323 --> 00:00:24.373
It is here you hear different views on how Danish society should be organised.

7
00:00:24.373 --> 00:00:29.490
The Folketing's debates on the content of laws are therefore very central to democracy.

8
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:37.115
In legislation, the Folketing can set the framework for Danish society and for citizens' daily lives.

9
00:00:37.065 --> 00:00:41.765
Both the government and the individual member of the Folketing can put forward bills.

10
00:00:41.765 --> 00:00:46.027
That is, propose a new law or propose to change an existing law.

11
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:51.453
In practice, however, the government puts forward by far most proposals for laws.

12
00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:53.828
It is often a big job to draft a bill.

13
00:00:53.828 --> 00:01:00.690
And the necessary knowledge and manpower are found especially among the civil servants in the government's ministries.

14
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:10.640
One or more members of the Folketing can also put forward a proposal for a Folketing resolution, a folketingsbeslutning, which obliges the government to draw up a bill.

15
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.380
A Folketing resolution, like laws, must be passed by a majority in the Folketing.

16
00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:23.205
The idea for a new law, or a change of an existing law, can come from many different places.

17
00:01:23.155 --> 00:01:31.755
The idea can, for example, originally come from a citizen, an association, an interest organisation or a municipality.

18
00:01:31.755 --> 00:01:34.980
They may draw attention to conditions they want changed.

19
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.905
That can then sometimes get a minister or a member of the Folketing to take up the matter.

20
00:01:39.905 --> 00:01:43.942
Other times it is a story in the media that sets the political agenda.

21
00:01:43.942 --> 00:01:50.055
It can for example be a TV programme that makes a politician propose a new law or a change in the law.

22
00:01:50.055 --> 00:01:56.430
Most initiatives do, however, come from the government itself, when it is to put its policy into effect.

23
00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:03.243
Part of the legislation that applies in Denmark also originates in various forms of international cooperation.

24
00:02:03.243 --> 00:02:06.462
Here especially new rules in the EU play a big role.

25
00:02:07.012 --> 00:02:12.024
A bill must go through three readings in the Folketing before it can finally be passed.

26
00:02:11.974 --> 00:02:16.450
The purpose is among other things to ensure that bills are thoroughly handled.

27
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.012
The third reading of the bill ends with a vote.

28
00:02:19.012 --> 00:02:22.037
If there is a majority for the bill, it is passed.

29
00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:25.149
If there is a majority against, it is rejected.

30
00:02:25.149 --> 00:02:29.149
So it is the Folketing, not the government, that adopts a bill.

31
00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:35.437
Votes are only valid if more than half of the members of the Folketing take part, that is at least 90 members.

32
00:02:35.437 --> 00:02:40.487
When the Folketing has passed a bill, both the king and a minister must sign it.

33
00:02:40.487 --> 00:02:44.616
After that, the law is published and can come into force.

34
00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:50.003
Now, direct democracy: referendums and citizens' proposals.

35
00:02:49.953 --> 00:02:59.241
The basic principle in Danish democracy is that the elected politicians are the population's representatives in the Folketing and in the other political assemblies.

36
00:02:59.241 --> 00:03:04.116
That means the politicians make the political decisions on behalf of the population.

37
00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:08.802
In return, the politicians must answer to the voters on election day.

38
00:03:09.352 --> 00:03:17.177
This representative democracy is sometimes supplemented by direct democracy in the form of referendums, folkeafstemninger.

39
00:03:17.127 --> 00:03:21.364
Here the population takes part directly in the democratic decision process.

40
00:03:21.364 --> 00:03:25.476
They can themselves decide whether they are for or against a specific bill.

41
00:03:26.026 --> 00:03:30.838
The constitution lays down that some questions must be decided by a referendum.

42
00:03:30.788 --> 00:03:36.488
That applies, among other things, if the constitution or the voting age is to be changed.

43
00:03:36.488 --> 00:03:46.113
A minority of the Folketing's members of at least one third, that is 60 members, can also demand that a passed bill be put to the voters for approval or rejection.

44
00:03:46.113 --> 00:03:55.226
There are also rules about a referendum when Denmark gives up its right of self-determination, its sovereignty, by taking part in international cooperation.

45
00:03:55.226 --> 00:04:11.613
If fewer than 150 of the Folketing's 179 members, but still a simple majority of at least 90 members, have said yes to Denmark taking part in the international cooperation, a referendum is held on whether Denmark should take part.

46
00:04:11.613 --> 00:04:17.046
This rule has become especially important in connection with Denmark's membership of the EU.

47
00:04:17.596 --> 00:04:23.908
Since the constitution was last changed in 1953, 16 referendums have been held.

48
00:04:23.858 --> 00:04:27.608
More than half of them have been about Denmark's relationship to the EU.

49
00:04:27.608 --> 00:04:39.446
In 2000, a majority, 53 percent, of the voters voted against introducing the common European currency, the euro, in Denmark instead of the Danish krone.

50
00:04:39.446 --> 00:04:44.696
There was a majority in the Folketing for introducing the euro, but the voters said no.

51
00:04:44.696 --> 00:04:58.133
In 2009, a majority, 85 percent, voted for equality between the sexes in the succession to the throne in the royal house, so that men and women have equal right of inheritance to the throne.

52
00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:09.246
In the latest referendum, in 2022, a majority, 66 percent, voted yes to abolishing the opt-out on the EU's defence and security policy.

53
00:05:09.246 --> 00:05:18.504
So since that referendum in 2022, Denmark no longer has an opt-out, a forbehold, on the EU's defence and security policy.

54
00:05:19.054 --> 00:05:28.266
Since 2018, citizens have also had the opportunity to get influence by putting forward so-called citizens' proposals, borgerforslag.

55
00:05:28.216 --> 00:05:38.054
This gives citizens who have the right to vote for the Folketing the possibility to put a proposal forward publicly on the website borgerforslag.dk.

56
00:05:38.054 --> 00:05:42.266
It takes at least four citizens to put forward a citizens' proposal.

57
00:05:42.266 --> 00:05:47.816
Other citizens with the right to vote for the Folketing then have the opportunity to support the proposal.

58
00:05:47.816 --> 00:05:57.754
If a citizens' proposal gets 50,000 supporters within 180 days, the Folketing considers whether the proposal should be put forward in the Folketing.

59
00:05:57.754 --> 00:06:02.629
Citizens are not entitled to have a citizens' proposal put forward in the Folketing.

60
00:06:02.629 --> 00:06:10.091
That is because the constitution says that only members of the Folketing and ministers can put forward proposals in the Folketing.

61
00:06:10.091 --> 00:06:15.866
Most citizens' proposals that have been put forward have not been able to gain a majority in the Folketing.

62
00:06:15.866 --> 00:06:31.216
The citizens' proposal on giving better rights to women giving birth, for example, to have the same midwife throughout the course and to stay overnight at the hospital after the birth, is however an example of a citizens' proposal that was passed in the Folketing.

63
00:06:31.216 --> 00:06:33.282
That happened in 2021.

64
00:06:33.832 --> 00:06:36.119
Here is your recap for Episode Four.

65
00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:50.044
Average turnout at general elections since 2000 is 86 percent, with a range of 84 to 88; Sweden 84, Norway 78, Germany 76, the United Kingdom 65.

66
00:06:50.044 --> 00:06:53.494
Elections must be held at least every four years.

67
00:06:53.494 --> 00:07:01.482
Elections are general, direct and secret, and you must be eighteen with Danish citizenship and a permanent residence in the realm.

68
00:07:01.482 --> 00:07:07.682
A party needs about 20,000 signatures, and an independent candidate 150 voters.

69
00:07:07.682 --> 00:07:11.282
You put one cross, either a personal vote or a party vote.

70
00:07:11.282 --> 00:07:14.032
Seats are distributed by the proportional method.

71
00:07:14.032 --> 00:07:21.469
The electoral threshold is two percent: Sweden and Norway four, Germany five, the Netherlands none.

72
00:07:21.469 --> 00:07:26.394
Since 1973, seven to twelve parties have been elected each time.

73
00:07:26.394 --> 00:07:33.257
A bill needs three readings, at least 90 members voting, and the signatures of the king and a minister.

74
00:07:33.257 --> 00:07:37.057
One third, 60 members, can demand a referendum.

75
00:07:37.057 --> 00:07:55.907
Since 1953 there have been 16 referendums, more than half about the EU: the euro rejected in 2000 with 53 percent, equal succession approved in 2009 with 85 percent, and the defence opt-out abolished in 2022 with 66 percent.

76
00:07:55.907 --> 00:08:03.084
Citizens' proposals started in 2018, and need 50,000 supporters within 180 days.

77
00:08:03.634 --> 00:08:06.234
Now it is time for your exam practice.

78
00:08:06.184 --> 00:08:11.096
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

79
00:08:11.096 --> 00:08:18.582
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

80
00:08:19.132 --> 00:08:20.382
Question 1.

81
00:08:20.332 --> 00:08:21.744
Who adopts a bill?

82
00:08:21.744 --> 00:08:23.994
Option A: The government.

83
00:08:23.994 --> 00:08:26.232
Option B: The Folketing.
[DA] Hvem vedtager et lovforslag?  A: Regeringen  |  B: Folketinget

84
00:08:32.782 --> 00:08:35.244
The answer is B: The Folketing.

85
00:08:35.194 --> 00:08:38.034
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Folketinget

86
00:08:38.584 --> 00:08:39.834
Question 2.

87
00:08:39.784 --> 00:08:45.221
Which subject has been the subject of the most referendums in Denmark since 1953?

88
00:08:45.221 --> 00:08:47.584
Option A: The constitution.

89
00:08:47.584 --> 00:08:49.846
Option B: The voting age.

90
00:08:49.846 --> 00:08:53.220
Option C: Denmark's relationship to the EU.
[DA] Hvilket emne har der været afholdt flest folkeafstemninger om i Danmark siden 1953?  A: Grundloven  |  B: Valgretsalderen  |  C: Danmarks forhold til EU

91
00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:03.345
The answer is C: Denmark's relationship to the EU.

92
00:09:03.295 --> 00:09:07.470
It appeared in the May 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Danmarks forhold til EU

93
00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.357
Question 3.

94
00:09:09.308 --> 00:09:14.308
Which of the Danish EU opt-outs was the subject of a referendum in 2022?

95
00:09:14.308 --> 00:09:17.920
Option A: The EU's common currency, the euro.

96
00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.670
Option B: The EU's common justice and immigration policy.

97
00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:26.736
Option C: The EU's defence and security policy.
[DA] Hvilket af de danske EU-forbehold var der folkeafstemning om i 2022?  A: EU's fælles valuta (euroen)  |  B: EU's fælles rets- og udlændingepolitik  |  C: EU's forsvars- og sikkerhedspolitik

98
00:09:33.286 --> 00:09:37.474
The answer is C: The EU's defence and security policy.

99
00:09:37.423 --> 00:09:40.026
It appeared in the May 2024 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: EU's forsvars- og sikkerhedspolitik

100
00:09:40.576 --> 00:09:41.876
Question 4.

101
00:09:41.826 --> 00:09:45.814
Does Denmark take part in the EU's defence and security policy?

102
00:09:45.814 --> 00:09:47.439
Option A: Yes.

103
00:09:47.439 --> 00:09:49.092
Option B: No.
[DA] Deltager Danmark i EU’s forsvars- og sikkerhedspolitik?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

104
00:09:55.642 --> 00:09:57.430
The answer is A: Yes.

105
00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.270
It appeared in the November 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

106
00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.283
Question 5.

107
00:10:02.233 --> 00:10:06.308
Does Denmark have an opt-out on the EU's defence and security policy?

108
00:10:06.308 --> 00:10:07.933
Option A: Yes.

109
00:10:07.933 --> 00:10:09.600
Option B: No.
[DA] Har Danmark forbehold for EU’s forsvars- og sikkerhedspolitik?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

110
00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.150
The answer is B: No.

111
00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.066
It appeared in the November 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Nej

112
00:10:21.616 --> 00:10:23.379
That's the end of this part.

113
00:10:23.329 --> 00:10:24.396
Well done.

